# Rank 1441 / CVE-2021-32702 (auth0/nextjs-auth0, GHSA-954c-jjx6-cxv7, CWE-79):
# reflected XSS through the SDK's default API-route error handler. The SDK
# mounts `/api/auth/[...auth0]` and wraps every one of its four handlers in
# `wrapErrorHandling` (`src/handlers/auth.ts`), which at the parent read
#
#   const wrapErrorHandling = (fn: NextApiHandler): NextApiHandler => async (
#     req: NextApiRequest,
#     res: NextApiResponse
#   ): Promise<void> => {
#     try {
#       await fn(req, res);
#     } catch (error) {
#       console.error(error);
#       res.status(error.status || 500).end(error.message);
#     }
#   };
#
# The message is not a constant. `/api/auth/callback` hands the request to
# openid-client's `client.callback(...)`; when the identity provider redirects
# back with an OpenID Connect `error`/`error_description` query parameter, that
# call throws an `OPError` whose `message` is built from those parameters, and
# `src/auth0-session/handlers/callback.ts` re-throws it as
# `throw new BadRequest(err.message)`. So a link to
# `/api/auth/callback?error=<script>…` puts attacker text into `error.message`,
# and `res.end(string)` writes it as the raw response body with no content type
# of its own — the browser sniffs it and runs the script on the application's
# own origin.
#
# The pool row is the GHSA merge commit and its combined diff is empty, the
# third known cause of that symptom (1382); `git diff 36655df8
# 6996e2528ceed98627caa28abafbc09e90163ccf` reads the change in full and needs no
# reconstruction, so the row is kept as the scan target and the parent is the
# fully vulnerable tree. The recorded CWE-79 is right. Metadata says
# repository and commit both check out.
#
# What the fixed revision does, and why it is invisible where the defect is rendered.
# The commit adds `htmlSafe` and a `HandlerError` class to `src/utils/errors.ts`
#
#   function htmlSafe(input: string): string {
#     return input
#       .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')
#       .replace(/</g, '&lt;')
#       .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')
#       .replace(/"/g, '&quot;')
#       .replace(/'/g, '&#39;');
#   }
#
#   export class HandlerError extends Error {
#     constructor(error: Error | AccessTokenError | HttpError) {
#       super(htmlSafe(error.message));
#       …
#     }
#   }
#
# and wraps each of the four handler factories in `try { … } catch (e) { throw
# new HandlerError(e); }`. `src/handlers/auth.ts` is not touched at all: the
# rendering line is byte-identical at `<fix>^` and `<fix>`, and so is
# `throw new BadRequest(err.message)`. The remediation is centralised in an
# error constructor two modules away from the write — the delegated-check shape
# recorded at 1371 and 1380 — so **no function-scoped fact discriminates the two
# revisions**, and this rank does not claim one. It is also not a complete
# neutralisation: `AccessTokenError` and anything else thrown outside the four
# wrapped factories still reaches the same `res.end(error.message)` raw, which
# is why the fixed revision also adds a README section telling anyone writing their own
# error handler not to render the message unescaped.
#
# What the shape is worth detecting anyway. "A caught exception's message is
# written into the HTTP response body" is precisely the review condition
# `code.UnescapedExceptionMessageMarkup` carries — "exception text is
# propagated into a markup-rendered error response without escaping", with the
# advisory "the exception message can include attacker-controlled request, URL,
# parser, or validation text and is later rendered in a browser". The concept
# is bound only in Java (`vyql/bindings/java/m420f6b67.vyql`,
# `vyql/bindings/packages/java/m9ef49b0f.vyql`); VYQL-INJ-033 consumes it at
# HIGH/CWE-79, so it is not one of the inert concepts (1385, 1426). This rank
# widens its language coverage to JavaScript/TypeScript rather than minting a
# sibling. Considered and rejected for reuse: `code.ErrorDetailDisclosure` and
# `code.VerboseHttpErrorObjectDisclosure`, which are about leaking internals
# rather than about markup; `code.HtmlRender`, which is the existing `res.send`
# sink concept and needs a taint source that does not exist for a `catch`
# binding; and a new sibling concept, which is what AGENTS.md calls ontology rot.
#
# `unescapedExceptionMessageResponseBody1180`
# (`vyql/bindings/javascript/mfb63017a.vyql`, appended at end of file, suffix
# well above the file's previous maximum of 1035) is written from Express and
# Next.js documentation only: `res.send`, `res.status(n).send`,
# `res.status(n).end`, `res.writeHead(n).end` are the documented body-writing
# calls, and `.message` is the documented `Error` property. Nothing in it names
# `HandlerError`, `htmlSafe`, `BadRequest`, `wrapErrorHandling` or any other
# identifier the commit introduces or touches; renaming all of them leaves the
# result unchanged.
#
# Four shaping decisions, each measured rather than assumed.
#
#  * **A bare `res.end` / `response.end` is excluded.** With it in the list the
#    parent reported 3 rows instead of 1: two extra on
#    `src/auth0-session/handlers/callback.ts`, whose function both re-throws
#    `err.message` and finishes an unrelated redirect with a no-argument
#    `res.end()`. The chained and `send` spellings always carry a body; a bare
#    `res.end()` usually terminates one. Cost, over the corpus enumeration
#    below: two lines in next.js's own test support code.
#  * **`res.write` is excluded** for the same reason and at the same price:
#    with it, n8n's `ai.controller.ts` streaming handlers contributed 2 rows for
#    `res.write(JSON.stringify(chunk))` beside an unrelated `e.message`.
#  * **An object-literal property carrying the message is declined**
#    (`not containsAny(node.context.prop, [".message"])`). Express serialises an
#    object body as JSON, so `res.status(500).send({ error: err.message })` is
#    not markup; without the clause librechat's `api/server/routes` reported 3
#    such rows, with it 0.
#  * **The neutralising control is read two ways**: an escaping or sanitising
#    call in the same function, and an HTML entity in a literal there — the
#    latter is what recognises the fixed revision's own `.replace(/&/g, '&amp;')` chain
#    when it is applied at the write rather than in a constructor. Both are
#    AST-derived token routes and are comment-proof: a function whose comments
#    say "escape", "sanitize", "encode", "&amp;" and "&quot;" still reports,
#    verified directly against the 1348/1402 prose-silencing defect.
#
# Measured. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB` over
# `src/`, three runs of each revision, identical every run: the parent goes
# 4 -> 5 findings, the one added row being VYQL-INJ-033 HIGH,
# `code.UnescapedExceptionMessageMarkup @ handlers/auth.ts:67`, which is
# `wrapErrorHandling`. The fixed revision reports the same 5, for the reason set out
# above. Without this binding no revision of this repository reported anything
# on either the rendering line or the `BadRequest(err.message)` line; the four
# pre-existing rows are two VYQL-CFG-014 on the session cache, one VYQL-RF-002
# in `frontend/`, and one VYQL-INJ-010 on cookie writing. Over the whole
# repository the parent reports 131 findings, 3 of them this binding's: the
# rendering line plus `tests/fixtures/setup.ts` and
# `tests/fixtures/test-app/pages/api/access-token.ts`, which are the SDK's own
# copies of the same default handler.
#
# The spec asserts the rule id rather than the concept: VYQL-INJ-033 fires and
# carries the concept's review text, but `code.UnescapedExceptionMessageMarkup`
# has no twin under `vyql/review/concepts/review/`, so it never appears in a
# spec's reviewed list.
#
# Reach and precision, enumerated rather than sampled. Across the 157 clones
# under /tmp/cve-work/rank*/ , an exhaustive grep for a response-body write of
# an `err`/`error`/`e`/`ex` `.message` in a `.js`/`.ts`/`.jsx`/`.tsx` file
# outside `node_modules` finds 30 lines in 9 repositories — that is the shape's
# whole reachable set in this corpus, and it is rare. Scanning the eight
# subtrees holding them: next.js `examples/` 8 rows, all of them Next.js API
# routes doing exactly this CVE's `catch { res.status(...).end(err.message) }`;
# n8n `packages/cli/src/controllers` 4, of which 2 are real
# (`binary-data.controller.ts` returns `res.status(400).end(error.message)` for
# a `BadRequestError` built from the caller's `id`) and 2 are co-occurrence
# artifacts on `project.controller.ts`, where a no-argument
# `res.status(201).send()` sits in a method that throws `BadRequestError(e.message)`;
# kubeflow pipelines `frontend/server` 3, one real
# (`res.status(500).send('Could not get main container logs: ' + err)`) and two
# artifacts of the same kind; mytube 3, all on one integration test's
# `res.status(err.status ?? 500).send(err.message ?? "Error")`, one row per
# nesting level of the wrapping lambdas (1412, 1414); librechat 0; graphql-http
# 0 (its instances are in doc comments, which produce no nodes). So roughly two
# thirds of the rows are the weakness and the rest are the co-occurrence
# residual.
#
# That residual is a named engine gap, not a shaping mistake: the JavaScript
# frontend emits no call-argument tokens at all (1360, reconfirmed 1388), so a
# function-scoped fact cannot tell that the `.message` it sees is the argument
# of the body write it also sees. Nothing in the data layer can close it, and
# faking the correlation is the workaround AGENTS.md forbids. Two of nest's
# instances are missed for a different known reason: `handleError` is a class
# **field** initializer arrow, which sits outside every method body (1325), so
# `packages/common/file-stream` reports 0.
#
# The three declines below are the discrimination. The first is the fixed revision's own
# escaping chain moved to the write; the second is the same remediation spelled
# with the documented `escape-html` package; the third answers with a JSON body,
# which is not a markup context at all.

test "rank1441: the default API-route error handler ends the response with a caught exception's message"
  lang javascript
  file src/handlers/auth.ts
  expect VYQL-INJ-033
  code
  ```
  import { NextApiHandler, NextApiRequest, NextApiResponse } from 'next';
  import { HandleLogin } from './login';
  import { HandleLogout } from './logout';
  import { HandleCallback } from './callback';
  import { HandleProfile } from './profile';

  const wrapErrorHandling = (fn: NextApiHandler): NextApiHandler => async (
    req: NextApiRequest,
    res: NextApiResponse
  ): Promise<void> => {
    try {
      await fn(req, res);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      res.status(error.status || 500).end(error.message);
    }
  };

  export default function handlerFactory({
    handleLogin,
    handleLogout,
    handleCallback,
    handleProfile
  }: {
    handleLogin: HandleLogin;
    handleLogout: HandleLogout;
    handleCallback: HandleCallback;
    handleProfile: HandleProfile;
  }) {
    return (userHandlers = {}): NextApiHandler => {
      const { login, logout, callback, profile } = {
        login: wrapErrorHandling(handleLogin),
        logout: wrapErrorHandling(handleLogout),
        callback: wrapErrorHandling(handleCallback),
        profile: wrapErrorHandling(handleProfile),
        ...userHandlers
      };
      return async (req, res): Promise<void> => {
        let {
          query: { auth0: route }
        } = req;

        route = Array.isArray(route) ? route[0] : route;

        switch (route) {
          case 'login':
            return login(req, res);
          case 'logout':
            return logout(req, res);
          case 'callback':
            return callback(req, res);
          case 'me':
            return profile(req, res);
          default:
            res.status(404).end();
        }
      };
    };
  }
  ```

test "rank1441: the same handler html-encoding the message before it becomes the response body"
  lang javascript
  file src/handlers/auth.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-033
  code
  ```
  import { NextApiHandler, NextApiRequest, NextApiResponse } from 'next';

  const wrapErrorHandling = (fn: NextApiHandler): NextApiHandler => async (
    req: NextApiRequest,
    res: NextApiResponse
  ): Promise<void> => {
    try {
      await fn(req, res);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      const body = error.message
        .replace(/&/g, '&amp;')
        .replace(/</g, '&lt;')
        .replace(/>/g, '&gt;')
        .replace(/"/g, '&quot;')
        .replace(/'/g, '&#39;');
      res.status(error.status || 500).end(body);
    }
  };

  export default wrapErrorHandling;
  ```

test "rank1441: the same handler passing the message through the escape-html package"
  lang javascript
  file src/handlers/auth.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-033
  code
  ```
  import escapeHtml from 'escape-html';
  import { NextApiHandler, NextApiRequest, NextApiResponse } from 'next';

  const wrapErrorHandling = (fn: NextApiHandler): NextApiHandler => async (
    req: NextApiRequest,
    res: NextApiResponse
  ): Promise<void> => {
    try {
      await fn(req, res);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      res.status(error.status || 500).end(escapeHtml(error.message));
    }
  };

  export default wrapErrorHandling;
  ```

test "rank1441: the same handler answering with a JSON body rather than a markup-rendered one"
  lang javascript
  file src/handlers/auth.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-033
  code
  ```
  import { NextApiHandler, NextApiRequest, NextApiResponse } from 'next';

  const wrapErrorHandling = (fn: NextApiHandler): NextApiHandler => async (
    req: NextApiRequest,
    res: NextApiResponse
  ): Promise<void> => {
    try {
      await fn(req, res);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
      res.status(error.status || 500).json({ error: 'internal_error', code: error.code });
    }
  };

  export default wrapErrorHandling;
  ```
